          "secNumber": "1.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 2: Field description and diagnostic combinations",
          "content": "### Mechanism and scientific meaning\n\nMineral recognition is probabilistic: color, habit, luster, streak, cleavage, hardness, and density provide overlapping but not individually decisive constraints. Surface weathering, trace substitutions and inclusions change appearance. Explain the mechanism at its appropriate scale: an atomic site, an ordered crystal, an individual grain, or a composite hand specimen. It matters whether the predicted feature belongs to a pure phase or reflects altered material surrounding it. A credible identification must distinguish the preferred phase from plausible alternatives, not merely find a familiar attribute.\n\n### What can be observed?\n\nScratch testing requires a reference standard and a fresh surface; streak is the powdered material color; cleavage gives repeatable crystallographically constrained surfaces. Document instrument or test settings and the specimen identifier alongside the result; a number without measurement context can be misleading. If the phase is small, intergrown or altered, question whether the observation volume is appropriately matched to the mineral grain. Describe a feature first, then separately provide the inference that it implies.\n\n### Case analysis with a rival explanation\n\n**Fictional setting:** A shiny yellow vein mineral has an uneven surface and a dark rim.\n\n**Different causal accounts:** Pyrite, chalcopyrite and weathered metallic materials are plausible; gold is possible but cannot be inferred from color alone.\n\n**A proposed discrimination:** Document hardness and streak carefully; use specific gravity and, if permitted, quantitative phase analysis.\n\nIf an observation seems to favor one account, ask what independent result would actually contradict that favored account. State whether a negative result would be decisive or might reflect detection limits, orientation or sample heterogeneity. Where the tests cannot discriminate the candidates, mark the identity unresolved rather than claiming a mineral name with unsupported certainty.\n\n### Limitation and consequence\n\nA camera white balance, contamination, or weathered patina can create a misleading color match. Good specimen labels distinguish what was seen from what was inferred, permitting future review. Describe the exact claim that is supported and the more ambitious claim that must remain provisional. A result from a selected grain need not represent the host rock; a bulk assay need not identify crystal structure; and a crystallographic result need not determine geological history by itself.\n\n### Worked quantitative example 2\n\n**Fictional problem:** A field log reports planar cleavage on 28 of 35 identifiable grains. Compute proportion with observed cleavage.\n\n**Inputs:** n = 28, N = 35. Use `100*n/N` to obtain **80 %**.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** A fraction in a selected sample is not a property of the entire exposure. A mathematical solution is useful only after its units, input precision and model domain are checked. Try changing one input by 10 percent and determine whether the answer varies linearly, inversely or nonlinearly; explain how the changed input would be measured in practice. The full-precision answer and formula are stored in `WORKED-CASES.json` for independent computation.\n\n### Practice and knowledge check\n\n1. Why can two specimens of the same mineral differ visually more than two different minerals?\n2. Make a three-column table: directly observed evidence, what it supports, and a rival explanation. Identify an observation that could genuinely discriminate the rivals.\n3. Recalculate the numerical example after modifying one input; include the original and new units and an appropriate uncertainty statement.\n4. Propose a feasible and safe supplementary test and specify how the sample or instrument might introduce error.\n5. In a short oral answer, name one conclusion that remains justified even if your preferred mineral identification is wrong.",
