          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 4: Density, habit and luster",
          "content": "### Mechanism and scientific meaning\n\nDensity depends on atomic masses, packing and voids; habit depends on growth conditions and symmetry; luster depends on electronic and surface interactions with light. Explain the mechanism at its appropriate scale: an atomic site, an ordered crystal, an individual grain, or a composite hand specimen. It matters whether the predicted feature belongs to a pure phase or reflects altered material surrounding it. A credible identification must distinguish the preferred phase from plausible alternatives, not merely find a familiar attribute.\n\n### What can be observed?\n\nMass divided by displaced volume approximates bulk density when the specimen is dry and the displacement measurement excludes trapped bubbles and water uptake. Document instrument or test settings and the specimen identifier alongside the result; a number without measurement context can be misleading. If the phase is small, intergrown or altered, question whether the observation volume is appropriately matched to the mineral grain. Describe a feature first, then separately provide the inference that it implies.\n\n### Case analysis with a rival explanation\n\n**Fictional setting:** A heavy, opaque cubic specimen contains small cavities and iron-stained surfaces.\n\n**Different causal accounts:** Galena may explain high density and cubic cleavage, yet a composite or porous fragment could bias the bulk density.\n\n**A proposed discrimination:** Compare mass-volume repeatability and, when appropriate, confirm chemistry without grinding or generating hazardous dust.\n\nIf an observation seems to favor one account, ask what independent result would actually contradict that favored account. State whether a negative result would be decisive or might reflect detection limits, orientation or sample heterogeneity. Where the tests cannot discriminate the candidates, mark the identity unresolved rather than claiming a mineral name with unsupported certainty.\n\n### Limitation and consequence\n\nConfusing a specimen density with single-crystal density can overstate identification certainty. A mineral property sheet should list the observed volume method, error bars and porosity caveat. Describe the exact claim that is supported and the more ambitious claim that must remain provisional. A result from a selected grain need not represent the host rock; a bulk assay need not identify crystal structure; and a crystallographic result need not determine geological history by itself.\n\n### Worked quantitative example 4\n\n**Fictional problem:** A dry specimen has mass 185 g and displaces 53 cm\u00b3. Compute its bulk density.\n\n**Inputs:** m = 185, V = 53. Use `m/V` to obtain **3.4906 g/cm\u00b3**.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** Trapped gas, porosity and water absorption bias the estimate. A mathematical solution is useful only after its units, input precision and model domain are checked. Try changing one input by 10 percent and determine whether the answer varies linearly, inversely or nonlinearly; explain how the changed input would be measured in practice. The full-precision answer and formula are stored in `WORKED-CASES.json` for independent computation.\n\n### Practice and knowledge check\n\n1. Why might apparent specific gravity of a hand specimen be lower than its constituent ideal mineral?\n2. Make a three-column table: directly observed evidence, what it supports, and a rival explanation. Identify an observation that could genuinely discriminate the rivals.\n3. Recalculate the numerical example after modifying one input; include the original and new units and an appropriate uncertainty statement.\n4. Propose a feasible and safe supplementary test and specify how the sample or instrument might introduce error.\n5. In a short oral answer, name one conclusion that remains justified even if your preferred mineral identification is wrong.",
