          "heading": "Lesson 20: Framework silicates, silica and feldspars",
          "content": "### Mechanism and scientific meaning\n\nTectosilicates share all four tetrahedral oxygen corners in a framework; silica end-members and aluminosilicate frameworks differ in substitution and compensating cations. Explain the mechanism at its appropriate scale: an atomic site, an ordered crystal, an individual grain, or a composite hand specimen. It matters whether the predicted feature belongs to a pure phase or reflects altered material surrounding it. A credible identification must distinguish the preferred phase from plausible alternatives, not merely find a familiar attribute.\n\n### What can be observed?\n\nFeldspar twins, optical signs and chemical stoichiometry complement diffraction; quartz can have conchoidal fracture and often lacks visible cleavage. Document instrument or test settings and the specimen identifier alongside the result; a number without measurement context can be misleading. If the phase is small, intergrown or altered, question whether the observation volume is appropriately matched to the mineral grain. Describe a feature first, then separately provide the inference that it implies.\n\n### Case analysis with a rival explanation\n\n**Fictional setting:** A weathered granitic rock contains several pale and glassy grains, some twinned and some untwinned.\n\n**Different causal accounts:** Quartz, alkali feldspar, plagioclase and secondary silica may be intergrown or partially replaced.\n\n**A proposed discrimination:** Compare texture, thin-section evidence and modal estimates from representative fields.\n\nIf an observation seems to favor one account, ask what independent result would actually contradict that favored account. State whether a negative result would be decisive or might reflect detection limits, orientation or sample heterogeneity. Where the tests cannot discriminate the candidates, mark the identity unresolved rather than claiming a mineral name with unsupported certainty.\n\n### Limitation and consequence\n\nA rock named \u201cgranite\u201d does not establish its exact mineral proportions without modal analysis. Framework silicates dominate much of the continental crust and underpin provenance inference. Describe the exact claim that is supported and the more ambitious claim that must remain provisional. A result from a selected grain need not represent the host rock; a bulk assay need not identify crystal structure; and a crystallographic result need not determine geological history by itself.\n\n### Worked quantitative example 20\n\n**Fictional problem:** If fully corner-sharing tetrahedra contribute 2 net O per Si, estimate net oxygen count for 12 Si centers.\n\n**Inputs:** Si = 12. Use `2*Si` to obtain **24 O atoms**.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** Framework charge balance depends on substitutions and cations. A mathematical solution is useful only after its units, input precision and model domain are checked. Try changing one input by 10 percent and determine whether the answer varies linearly, inversely or nonlinearly; explain how the changed input would be measured in practice. The full-precision answer and formula are stored in `WORKED-CASES.json` for independent computation.\n\n### Practice and knowledge check\n\n1. Why does Al substitution in a silica framework require additional charge balancing?\n2. Make a three-column table: directly observed evidence, what it supports, and a rival explanation. Identify an observation that could genuinely discriminate the rivals.\n3. Recalculate the numerical example after modifying one input; include the original and new units and an appropriate uncertainty statement.\n4. Propose a feasible and safe supplementary test and specify how the sample or instrument might introduce error.\n5. In a short oral answer, name one conclusion that remains justified even if your preferred mineral identification is wrong.",
