          "secNumber": "5.8",
          "heading": "Integrative investigation 5: A mixed carbonate\u2013sulfide specimen",
          "content": "### Research problem and scope\n\nAn educational specimen contains a pale matrix, a brassy opaque component, and a thin reddish surface coating. Treat all three as separate possible phases until testing demonstrates otherwise. Develop a non-destructive analytical plan: record high-resolution images, use a micro-XRF or equivalent supervised elemental map if available, and characterize carefully selected subsamples with powder diffraction or Raman analysis. Discuss why strong iron peaks in a bulk measurement cannot distinguish pyrite from an oxide coating. Model mass fractions using ideal stoichiometry, but keep mineral-phase proportions separate from whole-rock weight percent. Distinguish an ore occurrence from a resource or reserve: economics, recovery, cutoffs, legal permissions and environmental constraints lie outside the simple stoichiometric estimate.\n\n### Synthetic analysis and interpretation\n\nA companion investigation examines an evaporite with soluble and hydrated phases. Storage conditions can change humidity equilibrium, appearance and even the measured mass of a sample. No taste testing or unsupervised acid testing is permitted. Students compare hypothetical diffraction results before and after controlled climate exposure, without claiming a specific reaction solely from mass gain or loss. The discussion ends with an environmental reasoning exercise: sulfide presence may warrant assessment, but long-term drainage chemistry requires mineral abundance, oxygen and water access, reaction kinetics, neutralization capacity, hydrology and monitoring. A responsible mineralogist reports the limitations of the mineral data and refers decisions to qualified interdisciplinary teams.\n\n### Practical workflow, phase 1 \u2014 sampling and the question\n\nStart with the decision that the study is supposed to support: Create a safe, non-destructive identification and geochemical fate plan for a fictional mixed non-silicate sample. Make a numbered sampling plan rather than collecting data opportunistically. For each proposed specimen list selection criteria, sampling location or teaching provenance, predicted heterogeneity, the expected spatial scale of the relevant mineral property, and how the sample will be preserved. Selection effects are part of the result. If the question concerns a trace phase, one homogenized bulk measurement can destroy the very spatial context needed to locate the phase; if the question concerns average composition, selecting only the most interesting-looking grain can severely bias the answer. Explain how the objective determines whether you prioritize hand specimen, polished section, thin section, powder, or in-situ analysis.\n\n### Phase 2 \u2014 measurement, calibration and reproducibility\n\nPrepare a record of instrument or classroom method, date, operator, settings, standards, detection limits and data-processing steps. Put raw observations in one table and proposed mineral names in another. Record replicates rather than silently combining them; when results disagree, ask whether the difference is repeatability, spatial heterogeneity, true chemical zoning or changing ambient conditions. The fictional dataset in `datasets/unit-05.csv` demonstrates the mechanics of a tidy table without pretending to represent a geological location. Choose one column as a measurement proxy, sketch it against sample index, and identify whether its pattern justifies a mechanistic inference. An upward trend in arbitrary proxy numbers is not evidence of a geological reaction unless calibration, spatial metadata, and a physically meaningful causal model exist.\n\n### Phase 3 \u2014 competing explanatory models\n\nWrite a first hypothesis using the principal mechanism of this unit: Recognize non-silicate structures, reactions, economic importance, and environmental behavior. Then write a genuinely different hypothesis consistent with the same initial observations, motivated by this difficulty: Sulfides, oxides, carbonates, sulfates, phosphates, native elements, and halides differ in anion structure and chemical reactivity. Exposure to air and water can alter a surface before a geologist sees the original phase. Predict three distinct outcomes under each model. A useful discriminating observation is not merely \u201cmore data\u201d; it is a measurement whose possible results change the relative plausibility of the competing models. Look for possible confounding by sample alteration, orientation, unrecognized intergrowths, selection effects, data reduction, or unknown provenance. If the rival models yield identical predictions at the available scale, report non-identifiability rather than assigning a fabricated confidence percentage.\n\n### Phase 4 \u2014 quantitative reasoning with the five lesson calculations\n\nUse the worked problems from lessons 21\u201325 as transparent reference calculations. For each, reproduce the numeric answer and unit, then change one input and compute a sensitivity ratio. Record where a method assumes a pure single mineral, a cubic structure, a fixed section thickness, homogeneous mixing, or other simplifications. A reasonable mathematical curve is not a calibrated natural-system model. Review whether an expression is dimensionally consistent and whether it predicts nonsensical behavior near a boundary. If so, state the domain of validity and what physical effects the equation leaves out. Keep the numerical assumptions adjacent to the output chart to prevent reuse outside their teaching context.\n\n### Phase 5 \u2014 reporting and oral defense\n\nCreate a short abstract that states the question and the bounded conclusion. Attach specimen metadata, a simple observation matrix, a calculation table, a list of candidate phases, the diagnostic evidence, two remaining uncertainties and a prioritized next measurement. Provide raw measurements or enough metadata for an independent reanalysis; use sample identifiers that link photographs to laboratory measurements. Ask a second group to read the data without seeing the original conclusion and record whether they reach the same phase family, a different species, or a justified unresolved answer. In oral defense, identify the strongest contradicting observation and explain what would make you revise your preferred explanation. If practical environmental, engineering or economic consequences arise, clearly refer those judgments to qualified professionals and independently validated measurements.\n\n### Laboratory questions\n\n1. Which of the proposed tests is both least destructive and most likely to exclude a strong competitor? Justify the tradeoff.\n2. What is the main difference between analytical precision, phase identification accuracy and geological representativeness in this specific case?\n3. How could a small inclusion, alteration rim or unfavorable crystallographic orientation change the apparent result without changing the core mineral identity?\n4. Which inference is robust to losing one of the observations? Which collapses if a single measurement proves wrong?\n5. State a conclusion under three scenarios: predicted data, unexpectedly contradictory data and measurements below detection. Do not force a species name when evidence is ambiguous.",
