          "heading": "Lesson 36: Mineral assemblages in igneous rocks",
          "content": "### Mechanism and scientific meaning\n\nCrystallization sequence and melt composition shape igneous mineral assemblages, but actual rocks can contain accumulated crystals, xenocrysts, inherited cores and late alteration. Explain the mechanism at its appropriate scale: an atomic site, an ordered crystal, an individual grain, or a composite hand specimen. It matters whether the predicted feature belongs to a pure phase or reflects altered material surrounding it. A credible identification must distinguish the preferred phase from plausible alternatives, not merely find a familiar attribute.\n\n### What can be observed?\n\nTextures, mineral chemistry, phase relations and whole-rock mass balance together test igneous origins. Document instrument or test settings and the specimen identifier alongside the result; a number without measurement context can be misleading. If the phase is small, intergrown or altered, question whether the observation volume is appropriately matched to the mineral grain. Describe a feature first, then separately provide the inference that it implies.\n\n### Case analysis with a rival explanation\n\n**Fictional setting:** A basalt contains olivine crystals with unexpectedly Mg-rich cores and fine reaction rims.\n\n**Different causal accounts:** Early growth, crystal accumulation, magma mixing or disequilibrium entrainment are competing scenarios.\n\n**A proposed discrimination:** Map zoning, compare rim-melt equilibria and examine population-level grain textures.\n\nIf an observation seems to favor one account, ask what independent result would actually contradict that favored account. State whether a negative result would be decisive or might reflect detection limits, orientation or sample heterogeneity. Where the tests cannot discriminate the candidates, mark the identity unresolved rather than claiming a mineral name with unsupported certainty.\n\n### Limitation and consequence\n\nA simplified Bowen series is a teaching framework, not an exact thermal history of every magma. Mineralogy provides constraints on magmatic processes when integrated with petrology and geochemistry. Describe the exact claim that is supported and the more ambitious claim that must remain provisional. A result from a selected grain need not represent the host rock; a bulk assay need not identify crystal structure; and a crystallographic result need not determine geological history by itself.\n\n### Worked quantitative example 36\n\n**Fictional problem:** Modal estimate gives 22% olivine, 34% pyroxene and 44% plagioclase. Compute olivine+pyroxene fraction.\n\n**Inputs:** ol = 22, px = 34. Use `ol+px` to obtain **56 %**.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** Counting uncertainty and classification of mixed grains remain. A mathematical solution is useful only after its units, input precision and model domain are checked. Try changing one input by 10 percent and determine whether the answer varies linearly, inversely or nonlinearly; explain how the changed input would be measured in practice. The full-precision answer and formula are stored in `WORKED-CASES.json` for independent computation.\n\n### Practice and knowledge check\n\n1. How could a magma inherit crystals that did not crystallize from its final liquid?\n2. Make a three-column table: directly observed evidence, what it supports, and a rival explanation. Identify an observation that could genuinely discriminate the rivals.\n3. Recalculate the numerical example after modifying one input; include the original and new units and an appropriate uncertainty statement.\n4. Propose a feasible and safe supplementary test and specify how the sample or instrument might introduce error.\n5. In a short oral answer, name one conclusion that remains justified even if your preferred mineral identification is wrong.",
