          "secNumber": "8.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 37: Metamorphic minerals and pressure-temperature paths",
          "content": "### Mechanism and scientific meaning\n\nMetamorphic assemblages arise from reactions among bulk composition, pressure, temperature and fluid availability; index minerals can indicate metamorphic zones when properly contextualized. Explain the mechanism at its appropriate scale: an atomic site, an ordered crystal, an individual grain, or a composite hand specimen. It matters whether the predicted feature belongs to a pure phase or reflects altered material surrounding it. A credible identification must distinguish the preferred phase from plausible alternatives, not merely find a familiar attribute.\n\n### What can be observed?\n\nReaction textures, compositional zoning, mineral equilibria and assemblage maps constrain metamorphic conditions. Document instrument or test settings and the specimen identifier alongside the result; a number without measurement context can be misleading. If the phase is small, intergrown or altered, question whether the observation volume is appropriately matched to the mineral grain. Describe a feature first, then separately provide the inference that it implies.\n\n### Case analysis with a rival explanation\n\n**Fictional setting:** Garnet has an inclusion-rich core and an inclusion-poor rim accompanied by chlorite in the matrix.\n\n**Different causal accounts:** Growth during heating, later retrogression or local compositional heterogeneity may explain the association.\n\n**A proposed discrimination:** Trace inclusion geometry, zoning and reaction relationships before thermobarometric interpretations.\n\nIf an observation seems to favor one account, ask what independent result would actually contradict that favored account. State whether a negative result would be decisive or might reflect detection limits, orientation or sample heterogeneity. Where the tests cannot discriminate the candidates, mark the identity unresolved rather than claiming a mineral name with unsupported certainty.\n\n### Limitation and consequence\n\nOne mineral presence is not a unique pressure-temperature gauge without bulk-composition and equilibrium constraints. Metamorphic mineralogy reconstructs deformation and thermal histories across orogenic systems. Describe the exact claim that is supported and the more ambitious claim that must remain provisional. A result from a selected grain need not represent the host rock; a bulk assay need not identify crystal structure; and a crystallographic result need not determine geological history by itself.\n\n### Worked quantitative example 37\n\n**Fictional problem:** A garnet Fe fraction increases from 0.26 in core to 0.38 in rim. Find change.\n\n**Inputs:** core = 0.26, rim = 0.38. Use `rim-core` to obtain **0.12 fractional units**.\n\n**Physical interpretation:** Trend is not a uniquely calibrated temperature path. A mathematical solution is useful only after its units, input precision and model domain are checked. Try changing one input by 10 percent and determine whether the answer varies linearly, inversely or nonlinearly; explain how the changed input would be measured in practice. The full-precision answer and formula are stored in `WORKED-CASES.json` for independent computation.\n\n### Practice and knowledge check\n\n1. Why might two adjacent rocks at the same conditions have different mineral assemblages?\n2. Make a three-column table: directly observed evidence, what it supports, and a rival explanation. Identify an observation that could genuinely discriminate the rivals.\n3. Recalculate the numerical example after modifying one input; include the original and new units and an appropriate uncertainty statement.\n4. Propose a feasible and safe supplementary test and specify how the sample or instrument might introduce error.\n5. In a short oral answer, name one conclusion that remains justified even if your preferred mineral identification is wrong.",
